15:11:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's real historical truth buried in that, but I don't think it holds up as tightly as you're suggesting once you look at *how* American building actually diverged from British tradition. ## Where You're Right British colonists absolutely brought timber-framing knowledge to North America and Australia, and early colonial construction (17th-18th century) really was recognizably British — heavy timber frames, mortise-and-tenon joints, similar to what you'd see in an English barn or old cottage. ## Where It Actually Splits Off But American building practice didn't stay a continuation of British tradition — it **diverged into something distinct**, largely for practical/resource reasons, not cultural ones: - **Balloon framing** was invented in **1830s Chicago** — a genuinely American innovation using thin, standardized, machine-sawn lumber and nails instead of hand-cut joinery. This was a direct response to: - Vast, cheap timber supply in North America (unlike Britain, which had deforested much of its own timber centuries earlier and imported wood) - Industrialized sawmills producing dimensional lumber at scale - A shortage of skilled joiners/carpenters on the frontier — balloon framing let relatively unskilled labor build houses fast - This evolved into **platform framing** (the modern US/Canadian standard) by the early 20th century — again, a technical innovation, not an inherited British method So American timber construction isn't "British tradition carried over" so much as **British settlers started it, then Americans reinvented the method almost entirely** based on their own resource conditions. It's genealogically related, but not really "the same tradition" by the time you get to modern US framing. ## Australia's Case Is Messier Still Australian building was influenced by British settlers too, but also heavily shaped by climate adaptation (heat, termites in parts of the country) and, in modern decades, increasing use of brick veneer over timber frame — a hybrid approach not really mirroring UK practice either. ## The Honest Summary There's a real seed of truth — British settlers started the timber tradition in these countries — but calling modern US/Australian construction "basically British" glosses over a few hundred years of independent technical evolution driven by local resources, not inherited culture. It's less "still British when you get down to it" and more "distant cousin who moved away and became a very different person." That said — none of this really undermines your core point about the UK vs. Europe. That comparison stands on its own without needing the US/Australia argument at all.
