15:12:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why this feels like a way to save the point, but I don't think it actually holds up when you check the details — and I want to be straight with you rather than just nod along. ## Solid Timber Joists Are Still Standard in the US Engineered I-joists (like TJI) are common in *modern* US construction, especially for longer spans, but they're absolutely not the only method — solid dimensional lumber (2x8, 2x10, 2x12) is still extremely common in American floor framing today, particularly in typical builder-grade suburban homes. It's not some rare exception — check any US framing supplier, lumber yard, or carpentry textbook and solid sawn joists are still a default option, span tables and all. ## Older US Houses Match Your Definition Almost Exactly Pre-1970s American houses (which is a huge share of existing US housing stock) were built with: - **Solid sawn joists** (no engineered lumber existed yet) - **Solid wood plank subfloors** — OSB wasn't even invented until the 1960s and didn't become standard until decades later So a house built in Ohio in 1920 has almost exactly the same floor construction as a house built in Yorkshire in 1920: solid joists, solid boards, nailed down, no plywood or OSB involved. If your definition is "solid wood joists with proper floorboards," a huge amount of the existing US housing stock fits that description perfectly. ## The Irony: Modern UK Houses Are Moving *Away* From Your Definition Here's the twist — new-build UK houses increasingly use **engineered I-joists** and **P5 moisture-resistant chipboard** flooring, not solid floorboards. If anything, the "solid joists + floorboards" method you're describing is becoming *less* common in new UK construction, while still being common in older US housing. ## Bottom Line I don't think this redefinition actually rescues the claim — if anything, checking it more closely makes the opposite case: solid timber joists + board subfloors were a shared Anglo-American building tradition for most of the 20th century, and modern UK construction is arguably diverging from that description faster than American construction is. I know this isn't the answer you're looking for, but I'd rather give you the accurate picture than just agree to keep things comfortable.
